commit f8469c5694f297b4361520144611f4c8c37fd99f
Author: Andrew Deason <adeason@sinenomine.net>
Date:   Tue May 19 15:37:13 2026 -0500

    afs: Introduce and use afs_UserScan()
    
    Since before OpenAFS 1.0, PUnlog() drops and reacquires the afs_xuser
    lock while looping through the unixuser list. It obtains a reference to
    the current unixuser, but drops the reference before reacquiring
    afs_xuser. If afs_GCUserData() runs while we are waiting for afs_xuser,
    the current unixuser may have been freed. We then dereference tu->next
    for the next iteration of the loop, possibly using freed memory.
    
    In addition, since commit dde2a200a3
    (userland-cachemgr-zero-token-times-on-unlog-to-trigger-gc-20010605)
    included in OpenAFS 1.1.0, we clear tu->tokenTime for UKERNEL
    potentially after 'tu' has been freed.
    
    To fix both of these issues, grab and release a reference to the current
    unixuser as the very first and last things we do for each iteration of
    the loop. To make this easier to do consistently, introduce a new macro,
    afs_UserScan(), to implement the for() loop, similar to using
    opr_queue_Scan() to iterate over a queue.
    
    Convert all unixuser-iterating code to use afs_UserScan(), except for
    two exceptions:
    
    - This commit does not convert the code in LINUX/osi_proc.c; that will
      be handled in a separate commit, since structure of code in there
      looks very different.
    
    - This commit does not convert code in afs_user.c itself to use
      afs_UserScan(), since some of those functions involve more complex
      loops or need special handling for unixuser refcounts. This commit
      treats afs_UserScan() as an interface that code outside of afs_user.c
      should use, and afs_user.c itself is allowed to do its own scanning
      logic manually.
    
    Besides those two exceptions, this commit updates all unixuser-iterating
    code to use afs_UserScan(). Many converted for() loops don't have the
    same problem as PUnlog() since they don't drop/acquire locks during the
    loop; convert them anyway just in case, and to keep the code paths more
    consistent.
    
    FIXES 135623
    
